{"question": "A 35-year-old Caucasian female presents to the emergency room with unilateral leg swelling. She reports that she developed painful left-sided leg swelling and redness earlier in the day. She is otherwise healthy and takes no medications. She denies any recent prolonged travel. She experienced a similar episode affecting the opposite leg one year ago and was diagnosed with a thrombus in the right femoral vein. On examination, the left leg is erythematous and swollen. Passive dorsiflexion of the left ankle elicits pain in the left calf. Ultrasound of the leg reveals a thrombus in the left popliteal vein. A genetic workup reveals that she has an inherited condition. What is the most likely pathophysiology of this patient\u2019s condition?", "options": {"A": "Deficiency in protein C", "B": "Resistance to clotting factor degradation", "C": "Auto-antibodies directed against phospholipids", "D": "Thrombin inhibitor deficiency", "E": "Elevated serum homocysteine"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Hematology", "Vascular Medicine", "Emergency Medicine", "Genetics", "Internal Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Coagulation Disorders", "Thrombotic Disorders"], "question_analyses": {"Hematology": "The patient's presentation is consistent with a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in the left leg, which is a serious condition where a blood clot forms in one of the deep veins of the body, typically in the legs. The patient's history of a previous DVT in the right leg and the current symptoms of unilateral leg swelling, erythema, and pain, along with the positive finding of a thrombus in the left popliteal vein on ultrasound, strongly support this diagnosis. The most alarming and noteworthy aspects of this case include:\n\n1. **Recurrent DVT**: The patient has experienced DVT in both legs, with the most recent episode affecting the left leg. Recurrent DVTs are concerning as they indicate a high risk of future thrombotic events and suggest an underlyin